WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Robert Mugabe.

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:05.890
Hailed as an anti-colonialist hero; despised
as an autocrat and dictator.

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:11.710
At the time of this video production, Robert
Mugabe has just recently died, aged 95, after

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:15.490
37 years as the Prime Minister, and then President,
of Zimbabwe.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:19.390
The news of his death had barely become public
when many of you asked us to cover his life

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:20.830
story and career.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:22.510
Here, we’ve obliged.

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:27.630
Robert Mugabe’s rise to power is a similar
story to that of many rulers in the de-colonisation

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:28.630
period.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:34.670
A story of a struggle for independence, a
quest for national unity and societal development.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:43.070
And then: a gradual descent into autocratic
rule, economic chaos and violence.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:55.329
A Student and a Teacher
Robert Mugabe was born on the February 21,

00:00:55.329 --> 00:01:00.940
1924 in Kutama, not far from Harare, now the
capital city of Zimbabwe, but formerly the

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.899
administrative centre of Southern Rhodesia,
a recently constituted, self-governing British

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:06.899
colony.

00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:10.659
At that time, political and economic power
was firmly in the hands of the white minority,

00:01:10.659 --> 00:01:14.999
some 270,000 citizens who controlled the most
fertile land.

00:01:14.999 --> 00:01:19.159
Conversely, six million locals were pushed
into the colony’s driest regions, making

00:01:19.159 --> 00:01:21.509
a hard living from subsistence farming.

00:01:21.509 --> 00:01:25.889
Young Robert was educated in a school run
by catholic missionaries, and was by all accounts

00:01:25.889 --> 00:01:28.209
a studious and hardworking child.

00:01:28.209 --> 00:01:32.749
He later recalled that he enjoyed solitude,
while looking after a herd of cattle in the

00:01:32.749 --> 00:01:34.390
sole company of a book.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:38.029
Robert’s father, a carpenter, abandoned
the family when the boy was only 10 years

00:01:38.029 --> 00:01:39.029
old.

00:01:39.029 --> 00:01:42.259
That same year, Robert’s elder brother died,
allegedly from a poisoning, which plunged

00:01:42.259 --> 00:01:46.579
his mother into a deep depression, interrupted
only by violent mood swings.

00:01:46.579 --> 00:01:50.560
In addition to looking after their farm and
attending school, Robert had to look after

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:51.560
his mother.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.039
He developed a strong bond with her, to the
extent of being bullied for being a ‘mummy’s

00:01:55.039 --> 00:01:56.039
boy’.

00:01:56.039 --> 00:02:01.060
Robert continued his studies into higher education:
from 1950 to 1952, he attended Fort Hare Academy,

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.119
in South Africa, thanks to a scholarship.

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:09.490
In this period, he started to develop a political
conscience, influenced by socialism and anti-colonialism,

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:14.519
inspired by a desire for independence from
British rule and by a will to break down racial

00:02:14.519 --> 00:02:15.519
divisions.

00:02:15.519 --> 00:02:19.590
Apartheid was becoming a tangible reality
in Rhodesia and in neighbouring South Africa,

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:24.519
and Robert found inspiration in Gandhi, Nehru,
and the struggle for Indian self-rule.

00:02:24.519 --> 00:02:29.430
Robert eventually graduated as a teacher,
the first of many academic achievements: in

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.799
later years, he would attain degrees in law,
administration and economics.

00:02:32.799 --> 00:02:38.290
The same year, 1953, saw Southern Rhodesia
merge with the British protectorate of Northern

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:39.290
Rhodesia.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:43.969
The resulting Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland
was still under British colonial administration.

00:02:43.969 --> 00:02:48.150
Robert soon became ‘Mr. Mugabe’ to his
students, after taking a series of teaching

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:51.939
positions, first in the North of the federation
– a territory which later became Zambia

00:02:51.939 --> 00:02:53.500
– and then in Ghana.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.330
Mr Mugabe’s experiences in Ghana were life
changing.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:59.909
On a personal level, he met and courted a
colleague, Sally Hayfron.

00:02:59.909 --> 00:03:01.640
The two married in 1958.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:06.160
On a political level, Ghana was an eye opener
for the young teacher: the West African country

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.269
was already independent, a first taste of
life free from colonial rule.

00:03:10.269 --> 00:03:14.489
He was deeply impressed by the policies of
Ghana’s first president, Kwame Nkrumah,

00:03:14.489 --> 00:03:16.719
and his brand of ‘African socialism’.

00:03:16.719 --> 00:03:21.170
This ideology embraced established aspects
of socialism such as communal land ownership

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:26.639
and planned economy, but fused them with private
initiative, an acceptance of individualism,

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:29.819
and a revival of pre-colonial African culture.

00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:32.370
Mugabe returned to Rhodesia in 1960.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:36.969
Inspired by Krumah, and encouraged by Sally,
he took his first steps into anti-colonial

00:03:36.969 --> 00:03:37.969
activism.

00:03:37.969 --> 00:03:41.590
He started campaigning against the discrimination
of the black majority, which granted him an

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:45.660
invitation to speak at a rally of the National
Democratic Party, or NDP.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:50.439
The party leader, Joshua Nkomo, was impressed
by the teacher and hired him as publicity

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:52.150
secretary for the NDP.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:56.579
Unfortunately, this alliance of two strong
personalities was doomed to failure.

00:03:56.579 --> 00:04:03.431
A Slow Rise to 
the Top

00:04:03.431 --> 00:04:07.599
The leadership and membership of the NDP mirrored
the make-up of the black population in Rhodesia,

00:04:07.599 --> 00:04:13.469
as it was divided into two main ethnicities:
the Shona majority and the Ndebele minority.

00:04:13.469 --> 00:04:16.019
Nkomo, and most of the leaders, belonged to
the latter.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:21.470
In 1963, the Shona clans rebelled against
Nkomo, led by Reverend Ndabaningi Sithole

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:23.250
and by Mugabe himself.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:28.050
The reverend and the teacher formed a breakaway
faction, which became the Zimbabwe African

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:30.250
National Union, or ZANU.

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:35.090
The strong anti-colonial stance of the ZANU
became a cause of concern for Rhodesian authorities.

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:37.930
In 1964, they cracked down on the party leadership.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:42.030
Mugabe, Sithole and other activists were convicted
of acts of sedition and sentenced to harsh

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:44.699
prison terms that could extend up to 11 years.

00:04:44.699 --> 00:04:49.240
The bitterness of incarceration was only made
worse by news from outside: Mugabe’s only

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:54.210
child had died in Ghana in 1965, and he had
been denied leave to attend the funeral.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:57.889
During the same year, Rhodesia broke away
from the British Empire, becoming an independent

00:04:57.889 --> 00:05:00.639
country, albeit without international recognition.

00:05:00.639 --> 00:05:03.750
Power remained firmly in the hands of the
white minority.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:08.740
The struggle of Mugabe, the ZANU and the NDP
shifted in focus: the priority was no longer

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:12.819
independence from the British, but rather,
an established government led by the black

00:05:12.819 --> 00:05:14.509
majority in the country.

00:05:14.509 --> 00:05:18.370
While in prison, Robert Mugabe made the most
of this time by further advancing his education

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:20.210
and preparing for total leadership.

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:25.800
By the early 1970s, Mugabe was still playing
second fiddle to Rev. Sithole, but trouble

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:26.800
was stirring.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.800
Other party members, also incarcerated, accused
the Reverend of cooperating too closely with

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.949
white authorities, in exchange for prison
privileges.

00:05:34.949 --> 00:05:39.690
Sithole was eventually ousted and Mugabe swooped
in, becoming the head of ZANU.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:43.449
In 1975, after the end of his prison term,
Mugabe fled to Mozambique with the help of

00:05:43.449 --> 00:05:44.449
a white nun.

00:05:44.449 --> 00:05:48.610
There, he joined the ZANLA guerrilla force
that had been harassing the Rhodesian government

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:50.020
since 1964.

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:54.840
This was part of the Rhodesian Bush War, also
known as the Zimbabwe Liberation Struggle.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:59.590
Let me tell you, this was a very complicated
war, so get your notepads out.

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:03.729
On one hand, you had the Rhodesian Government,
hampered by UN sanctions because of the lack

00:06:03.729 --> 00:06:09.000
of international recognition, but supplied
in secret by the South African Government.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.750
On the opposite corner: the ZANLA, force,
or Zimbabwe African National Liberation Army,

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:17.479
affiliated with ZANU, the party of Sithole
and Mugabe.

00:06:17.479 --> 00:06:22.169
The ZANLA operated from Mozambique and was
supported and supplied by China.

00:06:22.169 --> 00:06:26.629
In the third corner, there stood ZIPRA, the
Zimbabwe People’ Revolutionary Army, operating

00:06:26.629 --> 00:06:27.819
from Zambia.

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:32.699
Their backers were the Soviet Union and their
political leader was Joshua Nkomo, who had

00:06:32.699 --> 00:06:34.400
now founded his new party – ZAPU.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:38.319
[Caption: Zimbabwe African People's Union]
Interesting to note: the ZANLA/ZIPRA dichotomy

00:06:38.319 --> 00:06:43.280
reflected the larger Sino-Soviet split, a
major turning point of the Cold War.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.050
But let’s stay grounded in Rhodesia.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:49.639
Mugabe did not actively participate in the
fighting; his role was more political.

00:06:49.639 --> 00:06:53.939
He was the voice of the ideology who would
motivate the fighters and swell their ranks

00:06:53.939 --> 00:06:55.940
through propaganda and recruitment.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:07:00.900
It is generally recognised that ZIPRA was
in fact more of an active military force,

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:06.130
while Mugabe’s ZANLA was more interested
in sowing dissent and disobedience inside

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:07.130
Rhodesia.

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:10.939
The complexity of the situation was thankfully
reduced when Mugabe and Nkomo were forced

00:07:10.939 --> 00:07:15.190
by their followers to accept an uneasy alliance,
the Patriotic Front.

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:19.500
The two leaders did not actively cooperate,
but simply accepted each other’s existence

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.669
and avoided infighting across their separate
factions.

00:07:22.669 --> 00:07:26.330
While the insurgency versus the Rhodesian
government continued, the stage was being

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:30.280
set for a future conflict between the two
rebel leaders.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:33.880
A CIA analysis noted how the two tried to
upstage each other: Mugabe visited Havana

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:39.310
in July 1977 to seek support from Fidel Castro,
and possibly extend a hand to the Soviets.

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:44.120
This worried Nkomo, who also flew to Cuba
in November of 1978 to prevent the rival from

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:45.580
snatching Soviet favors.

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:48.729
But the war was finally nearing its end.

00:07:48.729 --> 00:07:52.669
In late 1979 the British government mediated
a peace deal between the Patriotic Front and

00:07:52.669 --> 00:07:53.669
Rhodesia.

00:07:53.669 --> 00:07:57.389
This agreement put an end to the war, sanctioned
the independence of Zimbabwe and pushed the

00:07:57.389 --> 00:08:01.719
relatively obscure Mugabe front and centre
on the International stage.

00:08:01.719 --> 00:08:06.629
In early 1980 Mugabe returned from his exile
to Harare and called for the first democratic

00:08:06.629 --> 00:08:09.810
election of the newly independent State of
Zimbabwe.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:14.599
Mugabe was one of the candidates, of course,
and he became Prime Minister with a 57% majority.

00:08:14.599 --> 00:08:24.169
One step at a time, the former teacher had
reached

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:25.169
the top.

00:08:25.169 --> 00:08:28.310
The Rain and the Chaff
Was Mugabe’s victory a fair and transparent

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:30.210
one, as ruled by British observers?

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:35.100
Well, Mugabe had certainly built a following
as a liberation hero and he could rely on

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:36.690
the vote of the Shona majority.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:40.590
But it is also claimed that Mugabe’s guerrilla
fighters intimidated many voters into picking

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:45.700
the right candidate, by forcing them to attend
indoctrination sessions, called ‘pungwes’.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:48.810
Mugabe’s rule was marked by similar crafty
methods.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:53.060
His first rival, Joshua Nkomo, was invited
to join the newly formed cabinet, as Home

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:54.360
Affairs minister.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:55.440
Generous, right?

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:58.120
Well, yes, but actually, no.

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:02.750
Nkomo’s powers were purposefully reduced:
he was denied any authority over rural local

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:06.890
government and, very importantly, over the
Special Branches of the Police.

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:10.000
Nkomo was a minister with no muscle.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:15.170
This move was however in line with Mugabe’s
public face: the face of reconciliation.

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:17.560
The young State was rife with divisions.

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:18.740
Mugabe vs Nkomo.

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:20.720
Shona vs Ndebele.

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:22.400
Black vs White.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:25.640
And yet the Prime Minister invited everybody
to “Remain calm.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:28.780
Respect your opponents and do nothing that
will disturb the peace.

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:34.610
We must now all of us work for unity, whether
you have won the election or not”.

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:39.050
Speaking of Unity, Mugabe was steering Zimbabwe
into becoming a one-party state, under the

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:41.910
ZANU-Patriotic Front, or ZANU-PF.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:45.850
And a one-party state could not tolerate opposition.

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:50.970
So, despite all the grand talk and overtures
to former rivals, Mugabe was preparing to

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:55.380
crack down hard on Nkomo and his followers
in the Ndebele minority.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:10:00.260
In August 1981, Mugabe invited military instructors
from North Korea to train his crack troops,

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:01.260
the Fifth Brigade.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:04.370
They would be known as Gukurahundi, a Shona
phrase meaning:

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:08.810
“the rain which washes away the chaff”
This phrase would become the name of their

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:12.070
more infamous operation, as we’ll hear in
a minute.

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:16.701
In February 1982, a police inspection discovered
a cache of arms hidden in a farm, owned by

00:10:16.701 --> 00:10:18.510
one of Nkomo’s companies.

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:22.770
This was the perfect pretext to accuse Nkomo
of plotting a coup, and to remove him from

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:23.770
the cabinet.

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:28.640
Next, in January 1983, Mugabe unleashed his
full-scale offensive against Nkomo’s followers,

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:32.310
the Ndebeles living in Matabeleland, or western
Zimbabwe.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:36.370
The Fifth Brigade, the Rain, swept through
the region and washed away what Mugabe considered

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:37.670
to be the chaff.

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:41.490
According to a report from Zimbabwe’s Catholic
Commission for Justice and Peace, the Brigade

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:46.810
was responsible for mass murders, beatings
and property burnings in the areas where Nkomo’s

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:47.810
supporters lived.

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:52.750
Within six weeks more than 2000 civilians
had died, hundreds of homesteads had been

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:56.570
burnt and many more thousands of civilians
had been beaten.

00:10:56.570 --> 00:11:01.680
Most of the dead were killed in public executions
involving between one and 12 people at a time.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:06.000
“Villagers frequently report being forced
to sing songs praising ZANU-PF while dancing

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:16.000
on the mass graves of their families and fellow
villagers, killed and buried minutes earlier.”

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:21.800
The operation in the west escalated into a
full genocidal massacre: the atrocities continued

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:26.420
until December 1987, when Mugabe and Nkomo
reached another tentative reconciliation.

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:34.420
By then, it is estimated that between 20 and
80 thousand Ndebele civilians had been murdered.

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:41.800
Executive President
While the atrocities went on in Matabeleland,

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:44.590
Mugabe was consolidating his grip on power
in Harare.

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:48.560
In 1985 Zimbabweans had been called to vote
for a new Parliament.

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:53.720
The white minority was constitutionally granted
20 seats in the Chambers and in that year,

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:56.190
Ian D. Smith’s party won all 20 of them.

00:11:56.190 --> 00:12:00.390
Mr Smith had been the last white Prime Minister
of Rhodesia, one who had vowed that the black

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:02.560
majority would never rule his country.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.820
Perceiving this as a threat, Mugabe sought
to reinforce his power base as much as possible:

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:12.210
so, he dissolved what little remained of Nkomo’s
party and merged it with his own ZANU-PF.

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:17.340
With an unbeatable majority in Parliament,
Mugabe in 1987 moved to legitimise his lone

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:18.340
leadership.

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:22.370
He managed to win a majority vote ratifying
a change to the Constitution: this amendment

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:27.230
eliminated the largely ceremonial President’s
office, and created a new, much more powerful

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:30.420
position for himself – Executive President.

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:34.630
A title which combined the roles of Head of
State, Head of the Government and Commander-in-chief

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:35.730
of the Armed Forces.

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:40.860
In case some have not realized yet, Mugabe
had just legitimised his own dictatorship

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:42.490
over Zimbabwe.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:47.980
And yet, this was also his period of maximum
popularity, both at home and abroad.

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:52.080
Mugabe dedicated enormous slices of budget
to education and healthcare, his policies

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:55.420
managed to revive the economy and develop
urbanisation in the Country.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:13:00.100
He even introduced anti-corruption laws, known
as ‘leadership code’: this limited ZANU-PF

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:03.070
cadres from owning more than 50 acres of land.

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:06.700
Zimbabwe was rapidly developing and Mugabe
has also private affairs to take care of.

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:09.720
In January 1992 his wife Sally died of kidney
failure.

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:15.020
In 1996 Robert Mugabe married his second wife,
Grace Marufu, a former secretary in the presidential

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:18.000
office and 41 years his junior.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:22.560
Mugabe and Grace had had an affair since at
least 1990: their first child, a girl called

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:25.040
Bona was in fact born on that year.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:27.430
According to him, Mugabe had asked Sally’s
approval.

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:32.300
Their marriage was childless and he wanted
to sire a child before his own mother died.

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:35.640
Baby Bona could be counted among the so-called
‘Born-Frees’.

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:39.640
These were the new generation of Zimbabweans
born after the dissolution of Rhodesia.

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:43.480
Many of them were ready to enter the labour
market at the end of the 1990s.

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:47.150
Having benefited from Mugabe’s increased
spending on education, they were now realising

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:51.050
that the country could not offer them the
quantity and quality of jobs they were trained

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:52.050
for.

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:55.330
The Born-Frees lent their support to a new
party, the Movement for Democratic Change,

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:58.980
led by former labour leader Morgan Tsvangirai.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:02.280
Mugabe was about to face a new decade of turmoil.

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:10.150
Land grab
In February 2000 Mugabe called for a referendum

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:15.100
to ratify a new Constitution, which would
have solidified even more his grip on power.

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:19.800
The President was expecting an easy victory,
but the Movement for Democratic Change, more

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:23.370
and more popular, gave him defeat in the polling
stations.

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:27.670
Mugabe could not accept this and accused the
MDC members of being lackeys of the white

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:30.870
farmers, who had in fact openly financed the
party.

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:34.810
The white minority of farmers and entrepreneurs
made an easy target for the leader, who had

00:14:34.810 --> 00:14:39.080
always ruled on a platform of anti-colonialism
and anti-capitalism.

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:44.140
This minority was so small that it could hardly
pose a threat – about 70,000 citizens over

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:45.870
a total of 13 million.

00:14:45.870 --> 00:14:49.720
And yet Mugabe accused them of being dangerous
agents of British colonialism.

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:54.840
Things got even worse for Mugabe when the
June 2000 Parliamentary elections marked a

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:58.710
relative victory for the opposition: the MDC
did not win the majority of seats, but with

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:02.870
a good 57 out of 150 they could leave a mark
on legislation.

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:07.130
It seems like 2000 was the year in which the
President could not catch a break.

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:12.070
Another group raised their voices in dissent:
the veterans of the war of independence, theoretically

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:16.910
great supporters of Mugabe, but now disgruntled
as their pension funds had been embezzled

00:15:16.910 --> 00:15:18.200
by corrupt officials.

00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:22.900
The veterans sought compensation by forcibly
occupying farms, most of them operated by

00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:23.900
the white minority.

00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:27.480
Mugabe went with the flow, encouraging the
vets to claim the lands as their own.

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:30.850
From his perspective, this must have been
an easy alternative to actually having to

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:34.170
sort out an alternative pension fund!

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:38.161
Ironically, redistribution of land had been
one of the priorities of Mugabe’s government

00:15:38.161 --> 00:15:39.360
after independence.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:41.710
And yet the issue had just sat there for decades.

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:46.140
Neither Mugabe, nor the white farmers, not
even the British mediators, apparently were

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.150
interested in sorting it out.

00:15:48.150 --> 00:15:53.620
In 2000, a full twenty years after independence,
4,500 white farmers still owned more than

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:55.970
50% of Zimbabwean farmland.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:16:00.690
It seems like the uprising of the veterans
finally gave Mugabe the motivation to complete

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:03.420
the land reform – in the worst possible
way.

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:07.800
Mugabe’s tacit consent to occupy the farms
soon turned into active complicity.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:12.910
His supporters organised armed squads to invade
the farms and chase away their white owners.

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:17.090
Over the following two years, almost all the
white-owned farmland had been redistributed

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:19.310
to 300,000 black families.

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:23.360
Many of these farms were assigned to competent
commercial farmers, but many others went to

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:29.500
reward Mugabe loyalists, who had no experience
nor interest in working the land.

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:35.390
To use a Maoist analogy, this great agricultural
leap forward eventually caused more problems

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:36.430
than benefits.

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:40.820
Many farms fell into disuse due to lack of
modern equipment, fertilizers, efficient irrigation

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:42.880
or just plain incompetence.

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:46.740
Food shortages became common in the country,
although the Government painted them as a

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:48.520
consequence of drought.

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:52.900
Other leaders of the African Union started
to pressure Mugabe into revising his policies

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:54.340
or even retiring.

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:58.220
But Mugabe would cling like a barnacle to
his Presidential seat

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:02.040
“I am not retiring, I will never, never
go into exile.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:08.480
I fought for Zimbabwe, and when I die, I will
be buried in Zimbabwe, nowhere else.”

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:14.480
In 2008 a new season of elections proved to
the world how tough could this barnacle be.

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:18.909
In March, opposition leader Morgan Tsvangirai
claimed victory in a presidential vote.

00:17:18.909 --> 00:17:21.919
But Mugabe claimed that there had not been
an official vote count yet.

00:17:21.919 --> 00:17:25.480
This was delayed for weeks, until Mugabe’s
officials announced that there hadn’t been

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:26.620
a clear victor.

00:17:26.620 --> 00:17:28.899
The authorities scheduled a further round
of voting.

00:17:28.899 --> 00:17:33.309
But in the lead up to the vote Mugabe’s
security forces launched a campaign of intimidation

00:17:33.309 --> 00:17:35.870
against the opposition party, the MDC.

00:17:35.870 --> 00:17:39.370
This went as far as beating and killing opposition
supporters.

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:43.799
Tsvangirai had no choice but to seek refuge
in the Dutch Embassy in Harare, from where

00:17:43.799 --> 00:17:46.059
he announced his withdrawal from the election.

00:17:46.059 --> 00:17:48.659
In the subsequent round of voting, Mugabe
was the only candidate.

00:17:48.659 --> 00:17:51.750
Unsurprisingly, he won with 85% of the vote.

00:17:51.750 --> 00:17:55.809
As he had done in the past, Mugabe sought
to neutralise his opponent by absorbing him

00:17:55.809 --> 00:18:00.169
into the cabinet: Morgan Tsvangirai was sworn
in as Prime Minister, although it was clear

00:18:00.169 --> 00:18:04.690
that the executive and military power was
fully concentrated in Mugabe’s hands.

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:09.179
In the following 2013 elections, Mugabe, no
surprise, was the clear winner again.

00:18:09.179 --> 00:18:12.789
They may as well printed polling cards saying
“Who do you want as President?

00:18:12.789 --> 00:18:14.480
A) Robert Mugabe in a grey suit?

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:15.480
Or B)

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:18.010
Robert Mugabe in a blue suit?”.

00:18:18.010 --> 00:18:21.830
It is clearly both, as a blue suit is not
appropriate for evening social engagements.

00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:26.289
But the point is moot – by this time Mugabe
had famously ditched formal suits for colourful

00:18:26.289 --> 00:18:27.970
outfits and loud headgear.

00:18:27.970 --> 00:18:31.779
Whatever he wore after his victory, President
Mugabe decided to end his arrangement with

00:18:31.779 --> 00:18:34.450
Tsvangirai, which returned to the opposition.

00:18:34.450 --> 00:18:42.500
How to Ruin a Country
I am going to backtrack for a moment now to

00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:46.070
take a close look at Zimbabwe’s economy
under Mugabe in the early 2000s.

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:50.860
He had always been an autocrat, but at least
in the 1980s and 1990s some of his reforms

00:18:50.860 --> 00:18:53.929
had ensured better standards of living for
Zimbabweans.

00:18:53.929 --> 00:18:58.620
But after the ousting of the white farmers,
Mugabe’s economic policies became farcically

00:18:58.620 --> 00:18:59.620
incompetent.

00:18:59.620 --> 00:19:03.100
The collapse of Zimbabwe’s farming system
had a negative impact on the rest of the economy.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:08.470
As food became scarce, its price increased,
while farmers had less cash to spend on goods.

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:13.510
Moreover, Mugabe had involved his military
in Congo’s Civil War – a conflict so complex

00:19:13.510 --> 00:19:16.429
I will not even try to explain it.

00:19:16.429 --> 00:19:20.730
As the government got deeper and deeper into
debt, Mugabe’s central bank reacted in the

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:24.009
worst possible way: quantitative easy.

00:19:24.009 --> 00:19:28.690
That is, printing money – to pay off debts
and compensate the war veterans who had not

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:30.289
benefited from land distribution.

00:19:30.289 --> 00:19:35.490
As any citizen of the Weimar Republic could
tell you, this can only create catastrophic

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:41.570
inflation, which hit the rate of 231,000,000
percent.

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:46.049
The currency had to be issued in notes as
large as the 100 trillion Zimbabwe-dollar

00:19:46.049 --> 00:19:47.049
bill.

00:19:47.049 --> 00:19:48.049
In US dollars?

00:19:48.049 --> 00:19:49.549
That’s 40 cents!

00:19:49.549 --> 00:19:52.280
The increase in prices had also affected housing.

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:56.460
Many citizens in urban areas were unable to
pay rent and so were forced to live in improvised

00:19:56.460 --> 00:19:57.460
shelters.

00:19:57.460 --> 00:20:01.950
Mugabe’s solution was simply to get rid
of these shanty towns across Zimbabwe’s

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:02.950
major cities.

00:20:02.950 --> 00:20:07.231
This was called ‘Operation Murambatsvina’,
or “Drive out the rubbish” – a large

00:20:07.231 --> 00:20:12.700
scale Police operation which harassed, bullied
and coerced 700,000 residents into moving

00:20:12.700 --> 00:20:13.929
to the countryside.

00:20:13.929 --> 00:20:20.360
From 2008 to 2013, the coalition with Tsvangirai
had some positive effects in curbing inflation.

00:20:20.360 --> 00:20:26.600
But since Mugabe reasserted complete control
in 2013, he returned to his former inflation-inducing

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:27.600
policies.

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:30.779
How long could it last?

00:20:30.779 --> 00:20:37.860
A Bloodless Correction
In the second half of the 2010s unemployment

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:39.790
in Zimbabwe rose to 80 percent.

00:20:39.790 --> 00:20:43.920
The public health system, once the best in
Africa, had collapsed.

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:48.370
And yet Mugabe, Grace and their family appeared
to spend lavishly on luxury goods and travel

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:53.029
frequently to the Far East – both for shopping
sprees and to seek treatment in expensive

00:20:53.029 --> 00:20:54.029
private clinics.

00:20:54.029 --> 00:20:59.539
In 2014, apparently inspired by Grace, Mugabe
removed his vice-president Joice Majuru and

00:20:59.539 --> 00:21:02.929
replaced her with staunch loyalist Emmerson
Mnangagwa.

00:21:02.929 --> 00:21:07.499
This move coincided with the elevation of
Grace to a leadership post in ZANU-PF, the

00:21:07.499 --> 00:21:08.499
ruling party.

00:21:08.499 --> 00:21:12.659
It appeared as though Mugabe was setting the
stage for Grace to succeed him as the new

00:21:12.659 --> 00:21:13.870
Executive President.

00:21:13.870 --> 00:21:17.880
The first lady was not a popular candidate,
especially not amongst the military.

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:23.379
On the 7th of November 2017, shortly before
new presidential elections, Mugabe fired his

00:21:23.379 --> 00:21:24.429
vice-president.

00:21:24.429 --> 00:21:28.519
The military saw this a sign of Grace’s
impending appointment to the vice-presidency.

00:21:28.519 --> 00:21:32.610
One week later, the Generals occupied Harare,
arrested the Mugabes and placed them under

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:33.610
house arrest.

00:21:33.610 --> 00:21:39.029
It was clearly a coup, although military spokesmen
described it as a ‘bloodless correction’.

00:21:39.029 --> 00:21:43.409
Vice-President Mnangagwa was sworn in as President
in the same month.

00:21:43.409 --> 00:21:48.340
Robert and Grace were allowed to live in relative
calm inside their 24-bedroom home in Harare

00:21:48.340 --> 00:21:51.360
and even to travel frequently to Singapore.

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:55.140
Mugabe was being treated there for a long-term
illness, apparently cancer.

00:21:55.140 --> 00:21:59.749
Back in August 2019, President Mnangawa revealed
that Robert Mugabe had been hospitalised in

00:21:59.749 --> 00:22:01.320
Singapore for the past few months.

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:05.690
Then, on Friday the 6th of September Mnangawa
announced that former President Mugabe, now

00:22:05.690 --> 00:22:08.240
aged 95, had died in Singapore.

00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:14.019
“It is with the utmost sadness that I announce
the passing on of Zimbabwe’s founding father

00:22:14.019 --> 00:22:17.240
and former President, Comrade Robert Mugabe.

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:21.899
Mugabe was an icon of liberation, a pan-Africanist
who dedicated his life to the emancipation

00:22:21.899 --> 00:22:23.399
and empowerment of his people.

00:22:23.399 --> 00:22:28.759
His contribution to the history of our nation
and continent will never be forgotten.”

00:22:28.759 --> 00:22:34.669
On that we can agree: his contribution of
political violence, ethnic cleansing, corruption,

00:22:34.669 --> 00:22:51.659
and mismanagement will certainly

00:22:51.659 --> 00:23:01.259
not be forgotten.

